-- title:
--   Order of the width-weighted component group at supersingular nodes
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, and let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$. Work with the level-one modular function field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,K\,1$, the subfield of the Laurent series field over $K$ generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q)$ and $j(q^1)$. Let $W$ be a finite set of places of this field (a place being a proper valuation subring containing the image of $K$ and a principal ideal ring) which, by hypothesis, consists exactly of the supersingular places, i.e. of the places $w$ satisfying `IsSupersingularPlace p 1 K`. Index a weight function by the finite set $\mathrm{nodePairsOfPlaces}$ of pairs $(w,\ \varphi\cdot w)$, where $\varphi = \mathrm{arithFrobC}\,p\,K\,1$ is the semilinear automorphism acting by the $p$-th power Frobenius on the coefficients of Laurent series, and assign to the pair indexed by $w$ the weight $\mathrm{placeWidth}\,1$ of its first component, namely $\mathrm{jWidth}$ of the value of $j(q)$ at that place (which is $3$ at $0$, $2$ at $1728$ and $1$ otherwise) divided by $\mathrm{placeRamificationJ}\,1$. For this weight function $e$, the component group is the quotient of $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbb Z}(X,\mathbb Z)$, $X$ the sublattice of integer functions on the index set of total degree zero, by the image of the map sending $D \in X$ to the functional $D' \mapsto \sum_s e_s D_s D'_s$. The assertion is that this quotient has cardinality $\mathrm{eisensteinNumerator}\,p = (p-1)/\gcd(p-1,12)$.
--
--   This is the place-indexed form of the computation of the component group of the special fibre at $p$ of $J_0(p)$, whose order is the numerator of $(p-1)/12$ (Mazur–Rapoport, Theorem A.1), here phrased combinatorially in terms of the supersingular places of the level-one modular function field and the width weights at the crossing points. It is used by [`ModularCurve.natCard_componentGroup_widthOfPlaces_eq_eisensteinNumerator`](thm.html#ModularCurve.natCard_componentGroup_widthOfPlaces_eq_eisensteinNumerator).
